    blurb: "Say \"not\" the Indonesian way: tidak before verbs and describing words, bukan before nouns.",
    deck: "Traps for English speakers",
    body: `
<p>English has one little word for "not" and tucks it in after "am", "is" or "do": <i>I'm not hungry</i>, <i>I don't have a car</i>. Indonesian has no "am" or "do" to lean on, so it chooses its negative word by what comes right after it.</p>
<h3>tidak: not with verbs and describing words</h3>
<p>Put <b>tidak</b> straight before a describing word (hungry, hot, big) or a verb (live, have, go). Nothing else changes, and there is still no word for "am" or "is".</p>
{{ex:Saya tidak lapar.|SAH-yah TEE-dahq LAH-pahr|I'm not hungry.}}
{{ex:Kopi ini tidak panas.|KOH-pee EE-nee TEE-dahq PAH-nahs|This coffee isn't hot.}}
{{ex:Saya tidak punya mobil.|SAH-yah TEE-dahq POO-nyah MOH-beel|I don't have a car.}}
<h3>bukan: not with nouns</h3>
<p>When the thing after "not" is a noun (a teacher, my house, an American), Indonesian uses <b>bukan</b>. English says "I'm not a teacher"; Indonesian says <b>Saya bukan guru</b>, literally "I not-a teacher".</p>
{{ex:Saya bukan guru.|SAH-yah BOO-kahn GOO-roo|I'm not a teacher.}}
{{ex:Ini bukan rumah saya.|EE-nee BOO-kahn ROO-mahh SAH-yah|This isn't my house.}}
<p>A quick test: if you could put "a", "my" or "this" after the English "not", you want <b>bukan</b>. If you could put "very" there, or a verb, you want <b>tidak</b>.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>What follows</th><th>Negative word</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td>describing word</td><td class="k">tidak</td><td class="k">tidak mahal</td></tr><tr><td>verb</td><td class="k">tidak</td><td class="k">tidak tinggal di sini</td></tr><tr><td>noun</td><td class="k">bukan</td><td class="k">bukan teman saya</td></tr></table></div>
<h3>Short answers</h3>
<p>To answer no, echo the negative word the question needs. <b>Kamu lapar?</b> (Are you hungry?) gets <b>Tidak.</b> <b>Itu mobil kamu?</b> (Is that your car?) gets <b>Bukan.</b> Yes is <b>Ya</b>.</p>
{{ex:Itu mobil kamu? Bukan.|EE-too MOH-beel KAH-moo BOO-kahn|Is that your car? No.}}
<h3>Two more words for "not"</h3>
<p>You will meet two more soon. <b>belum</b> means "not yet", and it is the polite answer to many questions where English says a flat no. <b>jangan</b> means "don't" in a command: never use <b>tidak</b> to tell someone not to do something.</p>
{{ex:Belum.|buh-LOOM|Not yet.}}
{{ex:Jangan pergi!|JAH-ngahn puhr-GHEE|Don't go!}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> using <b>tidak</b> before a noun. <b>Saya tidak guru</b> → <b>Saya bukan guru</b>. And the reverse: <b>Dia bukan lapar</b> → <b>Dia tidak lapar</b>.</div>
`,
