    deck: "Numbers and money",
    body: `
<p>Indonesian numbers are built like Lego: learn ten words and a few building blocks, and you can say any price. You will need big numbers fast, because prices in rupiah run into the thousands.</p>
<h3>One to ten</h3>
<p><b>satu, dua, tiga, empat, lima, enam, tujuh, delapan, sembilan, sepuluh</b>. Zero is <b>nol</b>; in phone numbers people often say <b>kosong</b> (empty) instead.</p>
<h3>The building blocks</h3>
<p>English has special words like "twelve" and "twenty". Indonesian just adds a block: <b>belas</b> makes the teens, <b>puluh</b> means tens, <b>ratus</b> hundreds, <b>ribu</b> thousands, <b>juta</b> millions.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Number</th><th>Indonesian</th><th>Literally</th></tr><tr><td>12</td><td class="k">dua belas</td><td>two-teen</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td class="k">dua puluh</td><td>two tens</td></tr><tr><td>300</td><td class="k">tiga ratus</td><td>three hundreds</td></tr><tr><td>5,000</td><td class="k">lima ribu</td><td>five thousands</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:dua puluh lima|DOO-ah POO-looh LEE-mah|twenty-five}}
<h3>se- means "one"</h3>
<p>For "one" in front of a block, Indonesian uses <b>se-</b> joined to it, not <b>satu</b>: <b>sepuluh</b> (10), <b>sebelas</b> (11), <b>seratus</b> (100), <b>seribu</b> (1,000). For a million you hear both <b>satu juta</b> and <b>sejuta</b>.</p>
{{ex:sebelas|suh-buh-LAHS|eleven}}
{{ex:seratus ribu rupiah|suh-RAH-toos REE-boo roo-PEE-ahh|100,000 rupiah}}
<h3>Prices</h3>
<p>Prices are written with a dot for thousands: Rp25.000 is twenty-five thousand, not twenty-five. Read the number in chunks before the dot, then say <b>ribu</b>. To ask a price, say <b>Berapa harganya?</b> (literally "how much its price?").</p>
{{ex:Berapa harganya?|buh-RAH-pah hahr-GAH-nyah|How much is it?}}
{{ex:Harganya lima puluh ribu rupiah.|hahr-GAH-nyah LEE-mah POO-looh REE-boo roo-PEE-ahh|It's fifty thousand rupiah.}}
<h3>Numbers go before the noun</h3>
<p>Here is a surprise after all those noun-first lessons: numbers and words like <b>banyak</b> (many) come BEFORE the noun, just as in English. Describing words still follow the noun, so a number and a describing word end up on opposite sides. After a number the noun stays plain, never repeated.</p>
{{ex:tiga buku baru|TEE-gah BOO-koo BAH-roo|three new books}}
{{ex:dua rumah besar|DOO-ah ROO-mahh buh-SAHR|two big houses}}
<p>When you order food you will also hear the number after the dish, as a kind of shorthand. In an ordinary sentence the number comes first.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> using <b>satu</b> in front of a block. <b>satu ratus</b> → <b>seratus</b>, <b>satu ribu</b> → <b>seribu</b>.</div>
`,
