    blurb: "Ask whether something is there, say there isn't any, and ask a shop or hotel what they have.",
    deck: "Around town and directions",
    body: `
<p>English says "there is" and "there are", and asks "Is there…?" or "Do you have…?". Indonesian covers all of these with one short word, <b>ada</b>.</p>
<h3>ada: there is, there are</h3>
<p>Put <b>ada</b> in front of the thing. It never changes for one or many, or for past and present. A number still goes before the noun, and a describing word after it.</p>
{{ex:Ada dua kamar kosong.|AH-dah DOO-ah KAH-mahr KOH-sohng|There are two free rooms.}}
<p>To ask, just raise your voice at the end. The answer is the same word: <b>Ada.</b> (Yes, there is.)</p>
{{ex:Ada apotek di dekat sini?|AH-dah ah-POH-tehq dee duh-KAHT SEE-nee|Is there a pharmacy near here?}}
{{ex:Ada.|AH-dah|Yes, there is.}}
<h3>tidak ada: there isn't any</h3>
<p>For "there isn't" or "there aren't any", put <b>tidak</b> before <b>ada</b>. On its own, <b>Tidak ada.</b> is the short answer "No, there isn't."</p>
{{ex:Tidak ada air panas.|TEE-dahq AH-dah AH-eer PAH-nahs|There's no hot water.}}
{{ex:Tidak ada.|TEE-dahq AH-dah|No, there isn't.}}
<h3>ada for "do you have…?"</h3>
<p>In shops, cafes and hotels, Indonesians ask what is available with <b>ada</b>, not with a word for "have". The staff answer <b>Ada</b> or <b>Tidak ada</b>. To say you own something, use <b>punya</b>: <b>Saya punya mobil</b>.</p>
{{ex:Ada kamar kosong?|AH-dah KAH-mahr KOH-sohng|Do you have a free room?}}
<h3>ada before a place</h3>
<p>To say where someone is, you need no verb at all: <b>Dia di rumah</b>, "she's at home". You will also hear <b>ada</b> slipped in before the place, with the same meaning. Both are correct.</p>
{{ex:Dia ada di rumah.|DEE-ah AH-dah dee ROO-mahh|She's at home.}}
<h3>ada is not adalah</h3>
<p>They look alike, but they are different words. <b>ada</b> says that something exists or is somewhere. <b>adalah</b> only links two noun phrases in careful speech (<b>Ibu kota Indonesia adalah Jakarta</b>). It never means "there is", and it never goes before a place.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> reaching for <b>adalah</b> because it looks like "is". <b>Adalah apotek di dekat sini?</b> → <b>Ada apotek di dekat sini?</b></div>
`,
