    title: "The me- prefix",
    blurb: "Build the me- verbs for reading, writing, cooking, looking for and sending, with the right spelling.",
    deck: "Work and study",
    body: `
<p>The second big prefix is <b>me-</b>. Where a <b>ber-</b> verb usually stands alone, a <b>me-</b> verb usually does something TO something: you read a book, write a letter, cook rice. In careful speech these verbs wear their prefix.</p>
{{ex:Saya membaca buku.|SAH-yah muhm-BAH-chah BOO-koo|I'm reading a book.}}
{{ex:Kami memasak nasi goreng.|KAH-mee muh-MAH-sahq NAH-see GOH-rehng|We're cooking fried rice.}}
<h3>The prefix changes shape</h3>
<p>The tricky part is that the prefix ends in a nasal sound (m, n, ng or ny), and the nasal changes to fit the first sound of the root. English does something similar: "in-" becomes "im-" in "impossible". Here are the patterns with the verbs you need most.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Root</th><th>me- verb</th><th>English</th></tr><tr><td class="k">lihat</td><td class="k">melihat</td><td>to see</td></tr><tr><td class="k">baca</td><td class="k">membaca</td><td>to read</td></tr><tr><td class="k">cari</td><td class="k">mencari</td><td>to look for</td></tr><tr><td class="k">pakai</td><td class="k">memakai</td><td>to use, to wear</td></tr><tr><td class="k">tulis</td><td class="k">menulis</td><td>to write</td></tr><tr><td class="k">kirim</td><td class="k">mengirim</td><td>to send</td></tr><tr><td class="k">sewa</td><td class="k">menyewa</td><td>to rent</td></tr></table></div>
<ul><li>Before l, r, m, n, w, y the prefix is plain <b>me-</b>: <b>melihat</b>, <b>memasak</b>.</li><li>Before b it is <b>mem-</b>, and before c it is <b>men-</b>: <b>membaca</b>, <b>mencari</b>.</li><li>A root starting with p, t, k or s loses that letter, and the nasal takes its place: <b>pakai</b> → <b>memakai</b>, <b>tulis</b> → <b>menulis</b>, <b>kirim</b> → <b>mengirim</b>, <b>sewa</b> → <b>menyewa</b>.</li></ul>
{{ex:Dia menulis surat.|DEE-ah muh-NOO-lees SOO-raht|She's writing a letter.}}
{{ex:Saya mencari hotel murah.|SAH-yah muhn-CHAH-ree HOH-tehl MOO-rahh|I'm looking for a cheap hotel.}}
{{ex:Kami menyewa motor.|KAH-mee muh-NYEH-wah MOH-tohr|We're renting a motorbike.}}
<p>Some everyday <b>me-</b> verbs are best learned whole, such as <b>menyanyi</b> (to sing) and <b>mengerti</b> (to understand).</p>
<h3>Time and order stay the same</h3>
<p>The verb still never changes for time; time words go in front of it, and the thing after the verb keeps its own noun-first order.</p>
{{ex:Saya sedang membaca buku baru.|SAH-yah suh-DAHNG muhm-BAH-chah BOO-koo BAH-roo|I'm reading a new book.}}
<h3>In everyday speech</h3>
<p>Casual speech often uses the bare root: <b>Saya baca buku</b>. You will hear it constantly, and it is fine in conversation. In this course, typed answers use the full <b>me-</b> form, the one that is right everywhere.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> sticking the prefix on without changing it. <b>mengbaca</b> → <b>membaca</b>, <b>mempakai</b> → <b>memakai</b>, <b>mentulis</b> → <b>menulis</b>.</div>
`,
